<h1>Video 092 — DeadLock || Starvation</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Multi Threading Part-12 || DeadLock || Starvation</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/092-4U9FUlpvg_U.txt). ASR decoded in place: keep subing = keep writing (board); dead luck / luck = deadlock / lock; synchronizer / synchronizer keyword / synchronizer keyboard = synchronized; threats = threads; d one / v method / d1 = d1 method; d2 / to method = d2; last method = last; ch lock / as lock / ss luck / bs lock = A&#x27;s lock / B&#x27;s lock; religious / release = release; Inf wait = infinite waiting; r na y = right or not; ENT = interview; Control C = kill JVM process.</p>
<p>This lecture defines deadlock, stresses that synchronized is the cause (not the cure), shows there are no resolution techniques (only prevention), builds the classic A/B dual-lock demo on the board and as executable code (Deadlock / DeadlockDemo), proves removing any one synchronized removes deadlock, then contrasts deadlock vs starvation.</p>
<h3>00:06 — Topic: Deadlock (keep writing)</h3>
<p>Board heading: Deadlock. Interview-heavy. He jokes with the informal line “lock without key is nothing but deadlock / dead end — you cannot proceed further,” then moves to the programming definition.</p>
<h3>00:25 — Informal “lock without key” then programming definition</h3>
<p>Spoken informal: lock without key ≈ deadlock / dead end.</p>
<p>Programming definition (repeated until board is clear):</p>
<p>If two threads are waiting for each other forever, that type of infinite waiting is called deadlock.</p>
<p>Dialogue he acts out:</p>
<ul><li>Ask first thread: why waiting? → “I am waiting for second thread’s activity.”</li></ul>
<ul><li>Ask second thread: why waiting? → “I am waiting for first thread’s activity.”</li></ul>
<ul><li>Both wait for each other forever → deadlock.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// Board definition (dictated repeatedly):
// If two threads are waiting for each other forever,
 // such type of infinite waiting is called deadlock.</pre>
<h3>03:15 — Common wrong conclusion students give</h3>
<p>Student-style wrong statement: “By using synchronized keyword we can resolve / overcome deadlock.”</p>
<p>He rejects that. Correct teaching:</p>
<ul><li>synchronized is not the solution.</li></ul>
<ul><li>synchronized is the problem creator — the only reason a program enters deadlock in this classic model.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If you never use synchronized, your program never enters this kind of deadlock situation.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If there is no specific requirement, it is never recommended to use synchronized carelessly — it is a dangerous keyword; take special care.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// Board conclusion:
// synchronized keyword is the only reason for deadlock situation
 // (in the traditional synchronized model)
// → while using synchronized we have to take special care
// → if no specific requirement, never recommended to use synchronized</pre>
<h3>04:38 — Once program entered deadlock — what can programmer do?</h3>
<p>Once the program is in deadlock:</p>
<ul><li>As a programmer you cannot do anything useful inside the running process.</li></ul>
<ul><li>There are no resolution techniques for deadlock.</li></ul>
<ul><li>There are several prevention techniques.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Slogan: prevention is better than cure.</li></ul>
<ul><li>“Punishment” joke if you misuse synchronized: program hangs — “hanging by the hand.”</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
 // There are no resolution techniques for deadlock
 // but several prevention techniques are available
 // (prevention is better than cure)</pre>
<p>OS-level prevention details are not required for a Java programmer in this session. What is required: show how misuse of synchronized causes deadlock, with a program interviewers may ask you to write.</p>
<h3>07:32 — Rough design on board before code (class A / class B)</h3>
<p>Rough app he will convert to program:</p>
<p>Class A</p>
<ul><li>method d1 taking B type argument</li></ul>
<ul><li>method last</li></ul>
<p>Class B</p>
<ul><li>method d2 taking A type argument</li></ul>
<ul><li>method last</li></ul>
<p>Cross calls:</p>
<ul><li>Inside A’s d1: call b.last()</li></ul>
<ul><li>Inside B’s d2: call a.last()</li></ul>
<p>Create one A object and one B object. Two threads:</p>
<ul><li>Thread 1 calls a.d1(b)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Thread 2 calls b.d2(a)</li></ul>
<h3>10:00 — Case 1: methods NOT synchronized → no deadlock</h3>
<p>If d1, d2, last are normal (not synchronized):</p>
<ul><li>T1 gets chance → runs d1 → calls b.last() immediately → finishes → terminates.</li></ul>
<ul><li>T2 gets chance → runs d2 → calls a.last() immediately → finishes → terminates.</li></ul>
<ul><li>No deadlock — no locks held across the cross-call.</li></ul>
<h3>12:20 — Case 2: methods ARE synchronized → deadlock path</h3>
<p>If methods are synchronized:</p>
<ol><li>T1 calls d1 → needs lock of A → gets A’s lock → starts d1.</li></ol>
<ol><li>T2 calls d2 → needs lock of B → gets B’s lock → starts d2.</li></ol>
<ol><li>While executing d1, T1 must call b.last() which is synchronized on B → T1 needs B’s lock.</li></ol>
<ol><li>But B’s lock is currently held by T2 → T1 enters waiting for B’s lock, while still holding A’s lock.</li></ol>
<ol><li>While executing d2, T2 must call a.last() which is synchronized on A → T2 needs A’s lock.</li></ol>
<ol><li>But A’s lock is held by T1 → T2 enters waiting for A’s lock, while still holding B’s lock.</li></ol>
<pre class='code'>// Spoken lock story (decoded):
 // T1 holds A&#x27;s lock, waiting for B&#x27;s lock
 // T2 holds B&#x27;s lock, waiting for A&#x27;s lock
 // → both wait for each other forever → DEADLOCK</pre>
<p>He acts out the comic negotiation: “Please release your lock for one minute and I’ll give both locks” / “First give me your lock, then I’ll release in half a minute” — neither releases → deadlock.</p>
<h3>20:09 — Convert rough idea to executable code</h3>
<p>He starts writing the full program on board / IDE.</p>
<p>Class A — both methods synchronized:</p>
<pre class='code'>class A {
    public synchronized void d1(B b) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Thread-1 starts execution of d1 method&quot;);
        try {
            Thread.sleep(5000); // force T2 to start meanwhile
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
        System.out.println(&quot;Thread-1 trying to call B&#x27;s last method&quot;);
        b.last();
    }

    public synchronized void last() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Inside A, this is last method&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Class B — same pattern:</p>
<pre class='code'>class B {
    public synchronized void d2(A a) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Thread-2 starts execution of d2 method&quot;);
        try {
            Thread.sleep(5000); // intention: demonstrate deadlock
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
        System.out.println(&quot;Thread-2 trying to call A&#x27;s last method&quot;);
        a.last();
    }

    public synchronized void last() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Inside B, this is last method&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Why sleep(5000)? If there is no sleep, deadlock is not guaranteed (one thread might finish the whole cross-call before the other starts). Sleep makes both hold their object locks while the other is also sleeping holding the other lock.</p>
<h3>26:24 — Thread class that drives both sides</h3>
<p>He introduces a class extending Thread (names vary on board: MyThread → later renamed meaningfully to Deadlock / DeadlockDemo).</p>
<p>Need both A and B as instance fields so both main and child can use them.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Deadlock extends Thread {
    A a = new A();
    B b = new B();

    public void m1() {
        this.start();          // starts child thread → run()
        a.d1(b);               // executed by MAIN thread
    }

    public void run() {
        b.d2(a);               // executed by CHILD thread
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deadlock d = new Deadlock();
        d.m1();
        // Cannot put this.start() then a.d1(b) only in static main
        // without instance — a,b are instance variables
    }
}</pre>
<h3>28:26 — Thread count walkthrough after `this.start()`</h3>
<p>Before start: only main thread.</p>
<p>After this.start():</p>
<ul><li>Two threads exist.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Child executes run() → b.d2(a).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Main continues and executes a.d1(b).</li></ul>
<p>So:</p>
<ul><li>Main ≈ T1: holds lock of A, sleeps, then waits for B.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Child ≈ T2: holds lock of B, sleeps, then waits for A.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Both wait forever → deadlock.</li></ul>
<h3>31:22 — Why not put everything only in static main?</h3>
<p>If you try start then call d1 from static context without an instance, a and b as instance variables are not directly accessible. Hence the m1() instance method pattern (or create object and call).</p>
<p>He may rename class to Deadlock for a meaningful name — behavior unchanged.</p>
<h3>33:32 — Full board summary of the deadlock program</h3>
<pre class='code'>class A {
    public synchronized void d1(B b) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Thread-1 starts execution of d1 method&quot;);
        try { Thread.sleep(5000); } catch (InterruptedException e) {}
        System.out.println(&quot;Thread-1 trying to call B last method&quot;);
        b.last();
    }
    public synchronized void last() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Inside A, this is last method&quot;);
    }
}

class B {
    public synchronized void d2(A a) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Thread-2 starts execution of d2 method&quot;);
        try { Thread.sleep(5000); } catch (InterruptedException e) {}
        System.out.println(&quot;Thread-2 trying to call A last method&quot;);
        a.last();
    }
    public synchronized void last() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Inside B, this is last method&quot;);
    }
}

class Deadlock extends Thread {
    A a = new A();
    B b = new B();

    public void m1() {
        this.start();
        a.d1(b); // main
    }

    public void run() {
        b.d2(a); // child
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Deadlock().m1();
    }
}</pre>
<h3>33:59 — Experiment: remove ALL synchronized → no deadlock</h3>
<p>Remove synchronized from all four methods → compile and run:</p>
<pre class='code'>// Typical run (order may vary slightly):
Thread-1 starts execution of d1 method
Thread-2 starts execution of d2 method
Thread-1 trying to call B last method
Inside B, this is last method
Thread-2 trying to call A last method
Inside A, this is last method
// program ends — NO deadlock</pre>
<h3>34:47 — Experiment: only `d1` and `d2` synchronized; `last` not synchronized</h3>
<p>If only d1/d2 are synchronized and last methods are not:</p>
<ul><li>T1 holds A, calls b.last() — last is not synchronized → gets chance immediately → completes.</li></ul>
<ul><li>T2 similarly completes.</li></ul>
<ul><li>No deadlock (because the cross-call does not require the other object’s lock).</li></ul>
<h3>35:28 — All four synchronized → cursor blinks forever</h3>
<p>With all four synchronized + sleep: after the “trying to call last” lines, cursor keeps blinking — program hung.</p>
<pre class='code'>// prints (then hangs — cursor blinking):
Thread-1 starts execution of d1 method
Thread-2 starts execution of d2 method
Thread-1 trying to call B&#x27;s last method
Thread-2 trying to call A&#x27;s last method
// ... forever waiting — deadlock</pre>
<p>Only “solution” once hung: Ctrl+C / kill the program — not recommended as a real solution; there is no in-process resolution technique. “After 10 days you can come and the cursor is still blinking.”</p>
<h3>36:34 — Why sleep matters for the demo</h3>
<p>If you remove sleep:</p>
<ul><li>No guarantee of deadlock.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Possible schedule: main gets chance, runs entire d1 including b.last() and finishes in ~one second span, then child starts d2 — no overlapping lock hold → no deadlock.</li></ul>
<p>With sleep:</p>
<ul><li>T1 got lock of A and entered sleeping.</li></ul>
<ul><li>T2 got lock of B and entered sleeping.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Both hold locks; each then needs the other’s lock → deadlock shown reliably.</li></ul>
<h3>38:41 — He rewrites / cleans the same example again on board</h3>
<p>He redraws the same A/B/Deadlock program cleanly (same structure: synchronized d1/d2/last, sleep(5000) or sleep(6000), child via run, main via m1).</p>
<h3>44:44 — Expected output with blinking cursor noted</h3>
<p>Board output he wants students to write:</p>
<p>Thread-1 starts execution of d1 method
Thread-2 starts execution of d2 method
Thread-1 trying to call B&#x27;s last method
Thread-2 trying to call A&#x27;s last method
// cursor blinking  ← also write this on notes</p>
<p>“After 10 years also cursor is blinking” because both wait forever.</p>
<h3>45:58 — Critical interview conclusion about synchronized</h3>
<p>In this program: if we remove at least one synchronized keyword, the program will not enter deadlock.</p>
<p>Therefore again:</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board (dictated many times):
 // synchronized keyword is the only reason for deadlock situation
 // Due to this, while using synchronized keyword
 // we have to take special care</pre>
<p>(Interview ask: “What is deadlock? Explain with a program.” — this example.)</p>
<h3>48:13 — Next related word: Starvation (Deadlock vs Starvation)</h3>
<p>Deadlock vs Starvation — related OS/programming terms with a small minor difference.</p>
<h3>49:05 — Definitions contrasted</h3>
<p>Deadlock:</p>
<ul><li>Long waiting of a thread where waiting never ends.</li></ul>
<ul><li>That infinite long waiting is deadlock.</li></ul>
<p>Starvation:</p>
<ul><li>Long waiting of a thread where waiting ends at a certain point (maybe after a very long time — “10 years, 20 years, 100 years” — but it does end).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Long waiting that eventually ends = starvation.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
 // Long waiting of a thread where waiting never ends → DEADLOCK
 // Long waiting of a thread where waiting ends at certain point → STARVATION</pre>
<h3>50:07 — Example for starvation</h3>
<p>Best example he points to: a low-priority thread that keeps waiting while high-priority threads keep getting the CPU / resource. Waiting is long, but eventually (when high-priority work finishes) the low-priority thread may get a chance → starvation, not deadlock.</p>
<p>(Transcript has heavy ASR repetition on “low priority thread” through ~54:20; the teaching point is: low-priority thread starved by continuous high-priority activity.)</p>
<h3>51:37 — Difference restated</h3>
<ul><li>Deadlock: waiting never ends.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Starvation: waiting ends at a certain point.</li></ul>
<h3>54:42 — Close</h3>
<p>Students must have clear clarity on deadlock and starvation. End of Part-12.</p>
